-- CISSP (ISC2 2024) starter bank. 8 domains: 1 Security & Risk Management (16%),
-- 2 Asset Security (10%), 3 Security Architecture & Engineering (13%),
-- 4 Communication & Network Security (13%), 5 IAM (13%),
-- 6 Security Assessment & Testing (12%), 7 Security Operations (13%),
-- 8 Software Development Security (10%).
insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- Domain 1
('ci-f-001','cissp','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the CIA triad, and what complements it?$$,$$Confidentiality, Integrity, Availability. Often complemented by authenticity and non-repudiation. It is the foundation for classifying and protecting information.$$),
('ci-f-002','cissp','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the difference between due care and due diligence?$$,$$Due diligence is investigating and understanding risks (research, planning). Due care is acting on that knowledge with reasonable protective measures (implementation). "Diligence = detect, care = act."$$),
('ci-f-003','cissp','core',1,'1.3',$$What are the steps of quantitative risk analysis (SLE, ARO, ALE)?$$,$$SLE = Asset Value x Exposure Factor. ALE = SLE x ARO (annual rate of occurrence). ALE guides how much to justifiably spend on a control.$$),
('ci-f-004','cissp','core',1,'1.3',$$What are the four risk treatment options?$$,$$Mitigate (reduce), Transfer (insurance/contract), Avoid (eliminate the activity), Accept (formally bear it). Residual risk remains after treatment and must be accepted by management.$$),
('ci-f-005','cissp','core',1,'1.3',$$What is residual risk?$$,$$The risk remaining after controls are applied. Management - not the security team - must formally accept residual risk based on risk appetite.$$),
('ci-f-006','cissp','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the (ISC)2 Code of Ethics canon order?$$,$$1) Protect society, the common good, public trust, and infrastructure. 2) Act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, legally. 3) Provide diligent and competent service to principals. 4) Advance and protect the profession. Order matters for conflicts.$$),
('ci-f-007','cissp','core',1,'1.4',$$What is the difference between a policy, standard, procedure, and guideline?$$,$$Policy: high-level mandatory intent. Standard: mandatory specific requirements. Procedure: step-by-step how-to. Guideline: recommended, non-mandatory.$$),
('ci-f-008','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What is the difference between GDPR data controller and processor?$$,$$Controller determines the purpose and means of processing personal data; processor acts on the controller's instructions. Controllers bear primary accountability.$$),
('ci-f-009','cissp','core',1,'1.3',$$What is a Business Impact Analysis (BIA) and what does it produce?$$,$$Identifies critical business functions and the impact of their disruption over time; produces MTD, RTO, RPO, and recovery priorities that drive BCP/DRP.$$),
('ci-f-010','cissp','core',1,'1.3',$$What is MTD and how does it relate to RTO?$$,$$Maximum Tolerable Downtime is the longest a function can be down before unacceptable harm. RTO (recovery time objective) must be shorter than MTD.$$),
('ci-f-011','cissp','core',1,'1.2',$$What is the difference between a threat, vulnerability, exposure, and risk?$$,$$Threat: potential danger. Vulnerability: a weakness. Exposure: susceptibility to loss. Risk: likelihood a threat exploits a vulnerability and the resulting impact.$$),
('ci-f-012','cissp','core',1,'1.5',$$What is the difference between a law, regulation, and a standard like PCI DSS?$$,$$Laws are government mandates (criminal/civil); regulations implement laws; PCI DSS is a contractual industry standard - enforced by contract, not legislation.$$),
-- Domain 2
('ci-f-013','cissp','core',2,'2.1',$$What are the data lifecycle roles: owner, custodian, steward, processor?$$,$$Owner: accountable, sets classification. Custodian: implements/maintains protection. Steward: ensures data quality/context. Processor: processes on the owner's/controller's behalf.$$),
('ci-f-014','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What are the common data classification levels?$$,$$Government: Unclassified, Confidential, Secret, Top Secret. Commercial: Public, Sensitive/Internal, Confidential/Private, Restricted. Protection scales with classification.$$),
('ci-f-015','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What are the three data states and how is each protected?$$,$$At rest (disk/DB encryption), in transit (TLS/IPsec), in use (access controls, secure enclaves). Data in use is hardest to protect.$$),
('ci-f-016','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What is data remanence and how is it addressed?$$,$$Residual data left after deletion. Addressed by clearing (overwrite), purging (degaussing/crypto-erase), or destruction (shredding/incineration) per sensitivity.$$),
('ci-f-017','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What is the difference between scoping and tailoring a control baseline?$$,$$Scoping: removing controls that don't apply. Tailoring: adjusting/customizing the baseline (adding, modifying, compensating) to fit the environment and risk.$$),
('ci-f-018','cissp','core',2,'2.3',$$What is DLP and what does it protect?$$,$$Data Loss Prevention detects and blocks unauthorized movement of sensitive data across endpoints, network, and cloud based on classification and content rules.$$),
('ci-f-019','cissp','core',2,'2.4',$$What is the difference between clearing, purging, and destruction?$$,$$Clearing: overwrite for reuse in the same environment. Purging: stronger (degauss/crypto-erase) for release outside. Destruction: physical - highest assurance.$$),
('ci-f-020','cissp','core',2,'2.2',$$What is data sovereignty?$$,$$Data is subject to the laws of the country where it is physically stored/processed - driving where regulated data may reside.$$),
-- Domain 3
('ci-f-021','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the difference between the Bell-LaPadula and Biba models?$$,$$Bell-LaPadula protects confidentiality (no read up, no write down). Biba protects integrity (no read down, no write up). BLP = confidentiality, Biba = integrity.$$),
('ci-f-022','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What does the Clark-Wilson model enforce?$$,$$Integrity via well-formed transactions and separation of duties - subjects act on data only through controlled programs, enforcing consistency.$$),
('ci-f-023','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the difference between symmetric and asymmetric cryptography?$$,$$Symmetric: one shared key, fast, bulk data (AES). Asymmetric: public/private key pair, slower, key exchange and signatures (RSA/ECC). Hybrid uses asymmetric to exchange a symmetric key.$$),
('ci-f-024','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What does a digital signature provide?$$,$$Integrity, authenticity, and non-repudiation - a hash of the message encrypted with the sender's private key, verified with their public key.$$),
('ci-f-025','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the difference between encryption and hashing?$$,$$Encryption is reversible (confidentiality) with a key. Hashing is one-way (integrity), producing a fixed-length digest that cannot be reversed.$$),
('ci-f-026','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What is the TCB (Trusted Computing Base)?$$,$$The totality of hardware, software, and firmware that enforces security policy. Keeping it small and verified reduces the attack surface for the whole system.$$),
('ci-f-027','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What is a reference monitor and its properties?$$,$$The abstract mechanism mediating all access. Must be tamperproof, always invoked (non-bypassable), and small enough to be verifiable. The security kernel implements it.$$),
('ci-f-028','cissp','core',3,'3.1',$$What is defense in depth?$$,$$Layering multiple, diverse controls so the failure of one does not compromise the system - people, process, and technology across preventive, detective, and corrective types.$$),
('ci-f-029','cissp','core',3,'3.4',$$What physical security concept uses layered zones (CPTED)?$$,$$Crime Prevention Through Environmental Design - natural surveillance, access control, and territorial reinforcement, with defense-in-depth zones from perimeter to asset.$$),
('ci-f-030','cissp','core',3,'3.2',$$What is perfect forward secrecy?$$,$$Session keys are ephemeral, so compromise of a long-term private key does not decrypt past sessions - each session's key cannot be derived later.$$),
('ci-f-031','cissp','core',3,'3.3',$$What are the fire suppression classes relevant to data centers?$$,$$Class C (electrical) fires need non-conductive agents; clean agents (e.g., FM-200) or CO2 are preferred over water for equipment; pre-action sprinklers reduce accidental discharge.$$),
-- Domain 4
('ci-f-032','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What are the OSI model 7 layers?$$,$$Physical, Data Link, Network, Transport, Session, Presentation, Application. ("Please Do Not Throw Sausage Pizza Away.") Security controls map to layers.$$),
('ci-f-033','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the difference between TCP and UDP for security?$$,$$TCP is connection-oriented, reliable, and stateful (easier to filter statefully). UDP is connectionless, faster, and easier to spoof/amplify in DDoS.$$),
('ci-f-034','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the difference between IPsec AH and ESP?$$,$$AH provides integrity/authentication only. ESP provides confidentiality (encryption) plus integrity. Tunnel mode protects the whole packet; transport mode protects the payload.$$),
('ci-f-035','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What is network segmentation and why does it matter?$$,$$Dividing a network into zones (VLANs, subnets, DMZ) to limit lateral movement and contain compromise - foundational to zero trust and least privilege.$$),
('ci-f-036','cissp','core',4,'4.2',$$What is the difference between a stateful firewall and a packet filter?$$,$$A packet filter checks each packet against static rules. A stateful firewall tracks connection state, allowing return traffic and detecting out-of-state packets.$$),
('ci-f-037','cissp','core',4,'4.3',$$What are common secure protocol replacements?$$,$$Telnet->SSH, HTTP->HTTPS, FTP->SFTP/FTPS, SNMPv1/2->SNMPv3, LDAP->LDAPS, DNS->DNSSEC. Replace cleartext with authenticated/encrypted equivalents.$$),
('ci-f-038','cissp','core',4,'4.1',$$What is a converged/zero-trust network principle?$$,$$Never trust, always verify: authenticate and authorize every request by identity and context regardless of network location; assume breach; enforce least privilege per session.$$),
-- Domain 5
('ci-f-039','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What are the identity, authentication, authorization, and accountability steps (IAAA)?$$,$$Identification (claim identity), Authentication (prove it), Authorization (grant rights), Accountability (log actions). Each builds on the previous.$$),
('ci-f-040','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$What are the three authentication factor categories?$$,$$Something you know (password), something you have (token), something you are (biometric). MFA combines two or more different categories.$$),
('ci-f-041','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is the difference between DAC, MAC, RBAC, and ABAC?$$,$$DAC: owner decides. MAC: system-enforced labels/clearances. RBAC: permissions by role. ABAC: decisions from attributes (user, resource, environment).$$),
('ci-f-042','cissp','core',5,'5.2',$$What are FAR, FRR, and CER in biometrics?$$,$$FAR: false acceptance (impostor admitted - security failure). FRR: false rejection (valid user denied - usability failure). CER: crossover error rate where FAR=FRR, used to compare systems.$$),
('ci-f-043','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What is SAML and how does federated SSO work?$$,$$SAML exchanges authentication assertions between an IdP and SP. The user authenticates once at the IdP, which issues a signed assertion the SP trusts - passwords never reach the SP.$$),
('ci-f-044','cissp','core',5,'5.3',$$What is the principle of least privilege vs need to know?$$,$$Least privilege: minimum permissions to do the job. Need to know: access only to the specific information required for a task. Both limit exposure.$$),
('ci-f-045','cissp','core',5,'5.1',$$What is the identity lifecycle (provisioning to deprovisioning)?$$,$$Create/provision with least privilege, review/recertify periodically (attestation), adjust on role change, and promptly deprovision on departure to avoid orphaned accounts.$$),
('ci-f-046','cissp','core',5,'5.4',$$What is the difference between OAuth and OpenID Connect?$$,$$OAuth 2.0 is an authorization framework (delegated access). OpenID Connect is an authentication layer built on OAuth 2.0 (verifies identity/login).$$),
-- Domain 6
('ci-f-047','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the difference between a vulnerability assessment and a penetration test?$$,$$Assessment identifies and prioritizes weaknesses. Pen test actively exploits them to demonstrate real impact and chained attacks under agreed rules of engagement.$$),
('ci-f-048','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the difference between SOC 1, SOC 2, and SOC 3 reports?$$,$$SOC 1: financial reporting controls. SOC 2: security/availability/confidentiality/processing integrity/privacy (restricted). SOC 3: SOC 2 summary for public use.$$),
('ci-f-049','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is the difference between black-, white-, and gray-box testing?$$,$$Black-box: no prior knowledge (attacker view). White-box: full knowledge/source. Gray-box: partial knowledge. Trade-off between realism and coverage/speed.$$),
('ci-f-050','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the purpose of a code review and static analysis in testing?$$,$$To find security flaws early by examining source (manually or via SAST) before deployment - cheaper than fixing in production.$$),
('ci-f-051','cissp','core',6,'6.1',$$What is synthetic vs real user monitoring?$$,$$Synthetic (active) uses scripted transactions to test availability/performance proactively. Real user monitoring (passive) observes actual user interactions.$$),
('ci-f-052','cissp','core',6,'6.2',$$What is the difference between an internal, external, and third-party audit?$$,$$Internal: self-assessment by the org. External: independent auditor. Third-party: assessing a vendor/partner. Independence increases assurance.$$),
-- Domain 7
('ci-f-053','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What are the incident response phases?$$,$$Detection, Response, Mitigation, Reporting, Recovery, Remediation, Lessons Learned (ISC2 phrasing). Preparation underpins all; lessons feed back in.$$),
('ci-f-054','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What is the difference between RAID levels 0, 1, 5, 6, 10?$$,$$0: striping (no redundancy). 1: mirroring. 5: striping + single parity. 6: dual parity. 10: mirrored stripes (speed + redundancy).$$),
('ci-f-055','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What is the difference between full, incremental, and differential backups?$$,$$Full: everything. Incremental: changes since last backup (small, slow restore). Differential: changes since last full (grows, faster restore than incremental).$$),
('ci-f-056','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What is the difference between BCP and DRP?$$,$$Business Continuity Plan keeps the whole business running during disruption; Disaster Recovery Plan is the IT-focused subset restoring systems and data.$$),
('ci-f-057','cissp','core',7,'7.3',$$What are hot, warm, and cold recovery sites?$$,$$Hot: fully equipped, near-real-time data (minutes-hours, costly). Warm: equipment, stale data (hours-days). Cold: space/power only (days-weeks, cheapest).$$),
('ci-f-058','cissp','core',7,'7.1',$$What is the difference between an event, an incident, and a breach?$$,$$Event: any observable occurrence. Incident: an event that harms or threatens CIA. Breach: confirmed unauthorized access/disclosure of protected data.$$),
('ci-f-059','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$What is the order of volatility in evidence collection?$$,$$Most to least volatile: CPU cache/registers, RAM, network state, disk, then logs/backups. Collect the most volatile first.$$),
('ci-f-060','cissp','core',7,'7.2',$$What is separation of duties and job rotation?$$,$$Separation of duties splits critical tasks so no single person can commit and conceal fraud. Job rotation moves staff to detect irregularities and reduce dependence.$$),
('ci-f-061','cissp','core',7,'7.4',$$What is the difference between civil, criminal, and administrative law relevant to investigations?$$,$$Criminal: government prosecution, "beyond reasonable doubt." Civil: disputes, "preponderance of evidence." Administrative/regulatory: agency rules. Evidence standards differ.$$),
-- Domain 8
('ci-f-062','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What is the purpose of a Secure SDLC?$$,$$Embedding security in every phase (requirements, design, coding, testing, deployment, maintenance) so security is built in, not bolted on - "shift left."$$),
('ci-f-063','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is the difference between SAST and DAST?$$,$$SAST analyzes source/binary without running it (early, whole codebase). DAST tests the running application (real runtime behavior, later stage).$$),
('ci-f-064','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What are the top secure coding defenses for injection?$$,$$Parameterized queries/prepared statements, input validation, output encoding, and least-privilege database accounts. Never trust user input.$$),
('ci-f-065','cissp','core',8,'8.1',$$What is the difference between Waterfall, Agile, and DevSecOps for security?$$,$$Waterfall: sequential, security late. Agile: iterative, security per sprint. DevSecOps: security automated and continuous in the CI/CD pipeline.$$),
('ci-f-066','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is software configuration management and why does it matter?$$,$$Tracking and controlling changes to code, versions, and dependencies (versioning, baselines) so releases are reproducible, auditable, and free of unauthorized changes.$$),
('ci-f-067','cissp','core',8,'8.2',$$What is the risk of insecure deserialization?$$,$$Processing untrusted serialized data can lead to remote code execution or object injection - mitigate by validating, signing, or avoiding native deserialization of untrusted input.$$),
('ci-f-068','cissp','core',8,'8.3',$$What is a software composition analysis (SCA) tool for?$$,$$Identifying known-vulnerable and improperly licensed third-party/open-source components in your software supply chain.$$),
-- acronyms
('ci-a-001','cissp','acronym',1,'1.3',$$ALE$$,$$Annualized Loss Expectancy = SLE x ARO; expected yearly loss from a risk.$$),
('ci-a-002','cissp','acronym',1,'1.3',$$SLE$$,$$Single Loss Expectancy = Asset Value x Exposure Factor.$$),
('ci-a-003','cissp','acronym',1,'1.3',$$ARO$$,$$Annualized Rate of Occurrence - expected occurrences per year.$$),
('ci-a-004','cissp','acronym',1,'1.3',$$BIA$$,$$Business Impact Analysis - identifies critical functions and disruption impact.$$),
('ci-a-005','cissp','acronym',1,'1.3',$$MTD$$,$$Maximum Tolerable Downtime - longest a function can be down before unacceptable harm.$$),
('ci-a-006','cissp','acronym',7,'7.3',$$RTO$$,$$Recovery Time Objective - target time to restore a function (must be < MTD).$$),
('ci-a-007','cissp','acronym',7,'7.3',$$RPO$$,$$Recovery Point Objective - tolerable data loss measured in time.$$),
('ci-a-008','cissp','acronym',3,'3.3',$$TCB$$,$$Trusted Computing Base - all components enforcing the security policy.$$),
('ci-a-009','cissp','acronym',5,'5.1',$$IAAA$$,$$Identification, Authentication, Authorization, Accountability.$$),
('ci-a-010','cissp','acronym',5,'5.3',$$RBAC$$,$$Role-Based Access Control - permissions assigned via roles.$$),
('ci-a-011','cissp','acronym',5,'5.3',$$ABAC$$,$$Attribute-Based Access Control - access decided by attributes/context.$$),
('ci-a-012','cissp','acronym',5,'5.3',$$MAC$$,$$Mandatory Access Control - system-enforced labels and clearances.$$),
('ci-a-013','cissp','acronym',5,'5.3',$$DAC$$,$$Discretionary Access Control - resource owner grants access.$$),
('ci-a-014','cissp','acronym',5,'5.2',$$CER$$,$$Crossover Error Rate - biometric point where FAR equals FRR.$$),
('ci-a-015','cissp','acronym',7,'7.3',$$BCP$$,$$Business Continuity Plan - keeps the business running during disruption.$$),
('ci-a-016','cissp','acronym',7,'7.3',$$DRP$$,$$Disaster Recovery Plan - IT restoration subset of BCP.$$),
('ci-a-017','cissp','acronym',3,'3.2',$$PFS$$,$$Perfect Forward Secrecy - ephemeral keys protect past sessions.$$),
('ci-a-018','cissp','acronym',1,'1.5',$$GDPR$$,$$General Data Protection Regulation - EU personal-data privacy law.$$),
('ci-a-019','cissp','acronym',8,'8.1',$$SDLC$$,$$Software Development Life Cycle - phased process for building software securely.$$),
('ci-a-020','cissp','acronym',6,'6.2',$$SOC 2$$,$$Service Organization Control 2 - attestation on security/availability/confidentiality controls.$$),
-- feynman
('ci-y-001','cissp','feynman',1,'1.2',$$Explain the difference between due diligence and due care with a business example.$$,$$Model answer: Due diligence is doing your homework - researching and understanding the risks. Due care is acting responsibly on what you found. Example: before launching a product, due diligence is assessing the security risks, regulations, and threats. Due care is then actually implementing the controls, training, and policies a reasonable, prudent organization would. Courts look for both: you must have investigated (diligence) AND acted on it (care). Diligence without care is knowing and doing nothing; care without diligence is acting blindly.$$),
('ci-y-002','cissp','feynman',1,'1.3',$$Explain why residual risk must be accepted by management, not the security team.$$,$$Model answer: The security team implements controls, but they don't own the business consequences. After controls are applied, some risk always remains - the residual risk. Accepting it means agreeing to bear the potential loss, which is a business decision tied to the organization's risk appetite and budget. Only management has the authority and accountability to make that call. If the security team quietly accepted it, no one with real authority would have signed off on the exposure - so CISSP is emphatic: management formally accepts residual risk.$$),
('ci-y-003','cissp','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain Bell-LaPadula versus Biba in plain terms.$$,$$Model answer: Both are rules about reading and writing across security levels, but they protect opposite things. Bell-LaPadula protects secrets (confidentiality): you can't read above your clearance (no read up) and can't leak down by writing to a lower level (no write down). Biba protects trustworthiness (integrity): you can't be corrupted by reading lower-quality data (no read down) and can't contaminate higher-integrity data by writing up (no write up). Memory hook: BLP keeps secrets from leaking; Biba keeps bad data from flowing into good.$$),
('ci-y-004','cissp','feynman',7,'7.3',$$Explain how BIA outputs (MTD, RTO, RPO) drive the choice of a recovery site.$$,$$Model answer: The BIA tells you how long each function can be down (MTD) and how current the data must be (RPO), which sets the RTO you must hit. If a function's MTD is measured in minutes, you need a hot site - fully running with near-live data - because anything slower breaches the tolerance. If the MTD is a week, a cold site (just space and power) is cheaper and adequate. So the business analysis comes first and dictates the spend: you buy the cheapest recovery option that still meets the RTO/RPO the BIA demands, never the other way around.$$),
('ci-y-005','cissp','feynman',5,'5.3',$$Explain the difference between least privilege and need to know.$$,$$Model answer: They overlap but aren't identical. Least privilege is about capabilities: give a person the minimum permissions required to do their job - no admin rights if they only need to read. Need to know is about information: even within their permission level, they should only access the specific data a task requires. A doctor may have permission to view patient records (privilege), but ethically and legally should only open the records of patients they're treating (need to know). Together they shrink exposure from both the rights someone holds and the data they touch.$$),
('ci-y-006','cissp','feynman',8,'8.1',$$Explain why "shift left" security in the SDLC is cheaper and safer than testing at the end.$$,$$Model answer: A flaw found in design costs a fraction of one found in production - once code ships, fixing it means emergency patches, regression testing, downtime, and possibly a breach. Shifting left means building security into every phase: threat modeling in design, secure coding standards and SAST during development, testing before release. Bugs are caught when they're cheapest to fix and before attackers can reach them. Waiting until the end treats security as a gate to fail at, rather than a property built in - so you either ship insecurely or scramble expensively.$$),
('ci-y-007','cissp','feynman',6,'6.1',$$Explain to a manager why you want both a vulnerability assessment and a penetration test.$$,$$Model answer: They answer different questions. A vulnerability assessment gives you breadth - a prioritized inventory of weaknesses across your systems, so you know what to fix and in what order. A penetration test gives you depth and proof - it actually exploits weaknesses and chains them together the way a real attacker would, showing what damage is genuinely achievable. The assessment tells you what's theoretically wrong; the pen test tells you what's actually exploitable. Compliance often wants the assessment; the pen test tells you whether your defenses truly hold.$$),
('ci-y-008','cissp','feynman',7,'7.2',$$Explain separation of duties and why it prevents fraud better than trusting one skilled person.$$,$$Model answer: Separation of duties splits a sensitive process so no single person controls it end to end - for example, the person who requests a payment can't also approve and issue it. Even a completely trustworthy expert becomes a single point of failure: if they're compromised, coerced, or turn malicious, nothing stops them. By requiring collusion between two people to commit and conceal fraud, you make it dramatically harder and more detectable. It's not about distrusting individuals; it's about designing a process where trust isn't a single point that can fail silently.$$);
